Dust-Resilient Radiators for Megawatt Heat Rejection on Mars

Mathematical Framework, Architecture, and Technical Specification

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Final Version
Date: 26 April 2026

---

Abstract

Sustained megawatt‑class heat rejection on the Martian surface is a critical enabler for nuclear‑powered habitats, in‑situ propellant production, and high‑power electric propulsion. The principal threat to radiator performance is dust contamination, which degrades optical properties and rapidly pushes a system below its minimum heat‑rejection threshold. This work presents an integrated radiator concept that couples a bio‑inspired, adhesion‑reducing micro‑texture with active vibration shedding via shape‑memory alloy (SMA) actuators and a variable‑emissivity electrochromic layer, deployed from a compact origami flasher structure. A comprehensive mathematical framework is established: dust‑layer thermal resistance is proven negligible for all fractal morphologies up to the failure point; texture‑induced adhesion reduction is derived analytically from JKR contact mechanics and shown robust against tribocharging; passive wind cleaning is proven impossible under realistic dust‑devil spatial statistics; actuator sizing is based on a 99.9 % reliability criterion with full fatigue and power bounds; and a physics‑informed neural network (PINN) provides real‑time dust‑state estimation with formal error bounds that are propagated through a robust nonlinear model predictive controller. Architectural vulnerabilities—perchlorate‑induced hinge jamming, micrometeoroid penetration, electromagnetic crosstalk, and sensor lag—are resolved with specific, verifiable design requirements. The final 1 MWₜₕ system achieves a specific power of 170 W·kg⁻¹ after full AIAA‑S‑120 growth margins, stows within 2.5 m³, and is traceable from first‑principles mathematics to a 16‑point verification matrix. This baseline is suitable for formal Preliminary Design Review.

