I. Mathematical Framework

1.1 Definitions and Mars Environment

Definition 1.1 – Radiative Environment
Surface pressure P = 600~\text{Pa}; effective sky temperature T_{\text{sky}} = 3~\text{K}; ground temperature range T_g \in [150,270]~\text{K}; clear‑sky solar insolation G_{\text{solar}} \leq 400~\text{W/m}^2. The CO₂‑dominated atmosphere has a mean free path \lambda_{\text{mfp}} \approx 7\times10^{-6}~\text{m}, giving a Knudsen number \text{Kn} \in [0.01,0.1] for fin scales 10^{-4}–10^{-3}~\text{m}; continuum heat transfer is valid.

Definition 1.2 – Dust Loading Factor \Lambda
Dust is modelled as a log‑normal size distribution with median radius \bar{r}_d = 1.5~\mu\text{m} and geometric standard deviation \sigma_g = 1.5. The area fraction occluded, \Lambda(t) \in [0,1], evolves as

\frac{d\Lambda}{dt} = \dot{m}_{\text{dep}}(1-\Lambda) - \dot{m}_{\text{rem}}(u_{\text{wind}}, \lambda_{\text{text}}, a_{\text{act}}, \Lambda),
\]  

where the deposition rate \dot{m}_{\text{dep}} \approx 10^{-6}~\text{kg/m}^2/\text{s} during active dust lifting.

Definition 1.3 – Dust‑Devil Spatial Statistics
Dust devil occurrence at Jezero crater is \lambda_A \approx 3.4~\text{km}^{-2}\text{sol}^{-1} (peak season). The probability that a devil of core radius r intersects a 3200 m² radiator array is P_{\text{hit}}(r) = \min(1, A/(\pi r^2)). The observed radius distribution is p(r) \propto r^{-2} for 1 \le r \le 100~\text{m}.

