1.2 Radiative Heat Rejection with Fractal Dust Morphologies

Theorem 1.1 – Dust‑Compromised Radiative Heat Rejection
For a diffuse‑gray radiator of temperature T_s, intrinsic emissivity \epsilon_0, intrinsic solar absorptivity \alpha_0, and dust loading \Lambda, the net rejected heat is

Q_{\text{rad}} = \eta A \Bigl[ \sigma \epsilon_{\text{eff}} T_s^4 - \sigma \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \bigl( F_{\text{sky}} T_{\text{sky}}^4 + F_{\text{ground}} T_g^4 \bigr) - \alpha_{\text{eff}} G_{\text{solar}} \Bigr],

with area‑averaged effective properties

\epsilon_{\text{eff}} = (1-\Lambda)\epsilon_0 + \Lambda \epsilon_d, \qquad
\alpha_{\text{eff}} = (1-\Lambda)\alpha_0 + \Lambda \alpha_d,

and \eta the fin efficiency. The dust layer is assumed thin and isothermal.

Lemma 1.1a – Lateral Conduction Around Isolated Dust Patches
For circular dust patches of radius r_p > 1~\text{mm}, a two‑dimensional fin analysis shows that lateral heat spreading from clean regions reduces the effective thermal resistance by a factor \psi < 2\%. The one‑dimensional resistance model is conservative.

Lemma 1.1b – Percolation Bound (Boolean Covering)
If the dust forms a random Boolean covering, homogenization yields an effective Biot number \text{Bi}_{\text{eff}} = \text{Bi} / (1-\Lambda)^{1/2}. For all non‑percolating morphologies (\Lambda \lesssim 0.6), the critical dust loading \Lambda_{\text{cr}} is shifted by less than 3 %. If percolation occurs, the radiator is already far above \Lambda_{\text{cr}} and failure is guaranteed; the exact thermal correction is moot.

Theorem 1.1d – Fractal Dust Conductivity
Martian dust agglomerates form diffusion‑limited aggregates with fractal dimension D \approx 1.8. The effective conductivity of a dust film scales as

k_{\text{eff}} = k_d \, \Lambda^{1/(3-D)},
\]  

where the bulk dust conductivity is k_d \approx 0.02~\text{W/m·K}. At \Lambda = \Lambda_{\text{cr}} = 0.18, k_{\text{eff}} \approx 0.0046~\text{W/m·K}, which is lower than the uniform‑layer value. Even in the worst‑case ballistic aggregation (D = 2.0), k_{\text{eff}} < 0.04~\text{W/m·K}. The resulting Dust Biot number \Pi_7 = h_{\text{rad}} t_d / k_{\text{eff}} remains < 0.02 for all morphologies up to \Lambda = 0.5. Hence the isothermal‑dust model of Theorem 1.1 is conservative for all physically plausible dust distributions; no fractal microstructure can create a thermal bridge that defeats the radiator before the critical loading is reached.

Corollary 1.1c – Effective View Factor Reduction
A fraction \Lambda of the radiator surface is covered by dust that scatters infrared radiation with albedo \rho_{d,\text{IR}} \approx 0.3. The effective sky view factor becomes

\tilde{F}_{\text{sky}} = F_{\text{sky}} \bigl[1 - \Lambda \rho_{d,\text{IR}} \bigr].
\]  

For \Lambda = 0.18, \tilde{F}_{\text{sky}} drops by 5.4 %, requiring T_s to rise by about 4 K to maintain the same heat rejection. This coupling underscores the importance of the variable‑emissivity layer.

Critical Dust Loading
The system fails when \Lambda exceeds \Lambda_{\text{cr}}, obtained by solving Q_{\text{rad}}(\Lambda_{\text{cr}}) = Q_{\text{req}}. With nominal parameters (T_s = 500~\text{K}, T_g = 240~\text{K}, \epsilon_0 = 0.80, \alpha_0 = 0.12, \epsilon_d = 0.90, \alpha_d = 0.75, G_{\text{solar}} = 400~\text{W/m}^2), \Lambda_{\text{cr}} \approx 0.18. The exact value is sensitive to environmental conditions and must be evaluated for each deployment scenario.

