1.3 Adhesion and the Impossibility of Passive Cleaning

Theorem 1.2 – Texture‑Induced Adhesion Reduction
For a spherical dust particle on a sinusoidal surface of wavelength \lambda_{\text{text}} and amplitude h, the JKR pull‑off force is

F_{\text{ad, text}} = \frac{3}{2} \pi W R_{\text{eff}},
\]  

with effective radius

R_{\text{eff}} = \frac{r_d R_{\text{asperity}}}{r_d + R_{\text{asperity}}}, \qquad
R_{\text{asperity}} = \frac{\lambda_{\text{text}}^2}{4\pi^2 h}.
\]  

The adhesion reduction factor is

\phi \equiv \frac{F_{\text{ad, text}}}{F_{\text{ad, smooth}}} = \frac{2 R_{\text{eff}} f_{\text{geometry}}}{r_d},
\]  

where f_{\text{geometry}} \approx 0.66 accounts for non‑normal loading. For the chosen riblet geometry (\lambda_{\text{text}} = 50~\mu\text{m}, h = 15~\mu\text{m}, r_d = 1.5~\mu\text{m}), \phi \approx 0.28.

Lemma 1.2a – Dynamic Adhesion with Tribocharging
The total adhesion force includes a possible electrostatic image term:

F_{\text{elec}} = \frac{q^2}{16\pi\varepsilon_0 r_d^2}.
\]  

For typical Martian dust charges (q \approx 10^{-14}~\text{C}) and even in dust‑devil electric fields (\sim 10~\text{kV/m}), F_{\text{elec}} / F_{\text{JKR}} < 0.01. The worst‑case adhesion factor at end‑of‑life does not exceed \phi_{\text{EOL}} = 0.35. Passive wind cleaning remains impossible.

Theorem 1.3 – Probabilistic Impossibility of Passive Wind Cleaning
The critical wind speed for particle removal is

u_{\text{cr}}(r_d) = \sqrt{ \frac{2 \phi F_{\text{ad, smooth}}(r_d)}{C_D \rho_{\text{CO}_2} \pi r_d^2} }.
\]  

For the log‑normal size distribution, the probability of removal by a single gust of 30 m/s is < 10^{-3}. Incorporating the realistic dust‑devil spatial statistics (Definition 1.3), the expected mass removal rate is

\mathbb{E}[\dot{m}_{\text{rem}}] \approx 5\times 10^{-9}~\text{kg/m}^2/\text{s},
\]  

which is three orders of magnitude below the background deposition rate. Therefore \Lambda_{\text{eq}} \to 1, and passive cleaning is impossible in any meaningful expectation.

