1.4 Active Shedding with Statistical Reliability

Theorem 1.4 – Finite‑Time Dust Ejection
A panel of length L driven at frequency \omega with tip displacement \theta_0 L delivers tip acceleration a_{\text{tip}} = \omega^2 \theta_0 L. Detachment occurs when m_d a_{\text{tip}} > F_{\text{ad, text}}. The critical acceleration for a particle of radius r_d is a_{\text{min}}(r_d) = F_{\text{ad, text}} / m_d.

Corollary 1.4a – Statistical Shedding Threshold with Actuator Variance
To account for the full size distribution and ±15 % SMA torque variation, we design for the 99.9 th percentile requirement:

a_{99.9} = 1.3 \times 10^{5}~\text{m/s}^2.
\]  

This requires an SMA strain of 2.1 %, which yields a Coffin‑Manson fatigue life N_f > 5 \times 10^4 cycles—a margin > 40 over the mission’s 1200 cleaning cycles.

Lemma 1.4a – Power Budget with Thermal Decoupling
The mechanical power per 2 m panel at 2 Hz and 15 mm tip amplitude is 0.06 W (electrical ~1.2 W per burst). For 1600 panels, the burst power is 1920 W, but the average power over a 3‑sol cleaning interval is < 0.05~\text{W}. A Kapton thermal break ensures SMA wire temperature stays below 80 °C while the radiator operates at 500 K.

