II. System Architecture

2.1 Overview

The radiator system consists of three 20 m‑radius origami flasher units (total deployed area ≈ 3200 m²) constructed from a CFRP frame with compliant hinges and a 125 µm Ag/FEP film. Two antagonistic NiTi SMA wires per panel provide deployment locking and periodic vibration for dust shedding. A solid‑state WO₃/LiClO₄ electrochromic device (VEM) covers the entire radiating surface to modulate emissivity. A physics‑informed neural network estimates the unmeasurable dust loading \Lambda from temperature, wind, and photodiode signals, and a robust nonlinear model predictive controller adjusts coolant flow, VEM voltage, and actuation bursts to maintain the required 1 MWₜₕ rejection.

2.2 Hermetic Hinge Sealing

RQ‑ENV‑001a: All origami fold lines are sealed with a 25 µm Parylene‑C bladder, achieving a helium leak rate below 10^{-9}~\text{mbar·L/s}. This prevents perchlorate brine from wicking into hinges and freezing, eliminating a failure mode observed on the Mars Exploration Rovers. The seal is qualified by a 90‑day accelerated dust‑storm test (V‑006c).

2.3 Film Thickness and Micrometeoroid Protection

Theorem 2.2b – Hypervelocity Penetration Limit
Using the Cour‑Palais modified ballistic limit equation, the minimum FEP thickness to survive a 0.1 mm micrometeoroid at 20 km/s is 120 µm. The radiator film is therefore fixed at 125 µm (as in the baseline). Requirement RQ‑MMOD‑001 mandates that no single perforation exceeds 0.1 % of the total radiating area over the 10‑year mission.

2.4 Electrical Crosstalk Mitigation

To prevent SMA PWM switching from capacitively coupling into the VEM electrodes and causing delamination, all SMA and VEM power buses are galvanically isolated using optocouplers. This adds 0.2 kg per panel zone but eliminates the need for heavy low‑pass filters and meets MSL‑heritage EMC standards. Requirement RQ‑ELEC‑001 is verified by ripple measurements during actuation (V‑007).

2.5 Nonlinear Smith Predictor for Thermal Control

The radiator thermal time constant is ~300 s, but the embedded thermocouples have a 10 s lag. A nonlinear Smith predictor uses the full T^4 radiation law as internal model. Because \partial Q/\partial T_s > 0 everywhere, the predictor is globally monotonically stable. Gain scheduling around the nominal 500 K further ensures robust performance. The MPC horizon is 600 s with 10 s steps, and the robust tube radius is set to 150 K to absorb worst‑case PINN errors.

2.6 Reliability and Radiation Hardness

RQ‑REL‑002: The PINN runs on a Xilinx Zynq‑7000 FPGA in triple modular redundancy with error detection and correction scrubbing every hour. Heavy‑ion testing to 60 MeV·cm²/mg (V‑009) guarantees fewer than one unrecoverable error per year. The SMA drivers are dual‑redundant, and the VEM zones are operated in a 2‑out‑of‑3 voting scheme to prevent single‑point failures from dropping the heat rejection below 0.8 MW.

