Lemma A.5.1a — Lipschitz Propagation of Estimation Error (Lemma 1.5a)

The radiator plant dynamics are Lipschitz in \Lambda with constant L_f \approx 4.8 \times 10^{4}. Hence

\|T_s(t;\Lambda) - T_s(t;\hat{\Lambda})\| \le L_f \|\Lambda - \hat{\Lambda}\|_\infty. \tag{A.16}

A PINN error of 0.005 induces a temperature uncertainty of \Delta T_s \le 240\ \text{K}. The model predictive controller is equipped with a robust tube of radius 150 K and a nonlinear Smith predictor (see Appendix B.4) to guarantee constraint satisfaction.

---

A.6 Synergistic Material Degradation

Theorem A.6.1 — UV + Perchlorate Degradation of FEP (Theorem 1.6)

The rate of increase of solar absorptance is

\frac{d\alpha_0}{dt} = k_{UV} I_{UV} + k_{Cl}[\text{ClO}_4^-] I_{UV}. \tag{A.17}

With [\text{ClO}_4^-] \approx 0.5\ \text{wt}\%, I_{UV} \approx 0.1\ \text{W/m}^2, and k_{Cl}/k_{UV} \approx 8, the degradation time constant drops from 3000 sols (UV only) to approximately 400 sols. End‑of‑mission \alpha_0 can reach 0.30–0.35. The variable‑emissivity layer must therefore cover 100 % of the radiator area to compensate for this degradation.

