Appendix B — Architecture Design Details

B.1 Origami Deployment and Hermetic Hinge Sealing

Theorem B.1.1 — Deployment Torque Margin (Theorem 2.1, revised)

The required deployment torque is

M_{\text{req}}(T) = k_{\text{crease}}(T)\theta_{\text{fold}} + M_{\text{FEP,visco}}(T) + M_{\text{dust,jamming}}. \tag{B.1}

At the coldest deployment condition (-100^\circ\text{C}), the SMA wire (locally heated above A_f = 60^\circ\text{C}) delivers M_{\text{SMA}} \approx 0.15\ \text{N·m}. To eliminate the perchlorate‑induced jamming term, all fold lines are sealed with a 25 µm Parylene‑C bladder (RQ‑ENV‑001a). Helium leak rate is < 10^{-9}\ \text{mbar·L/s}, verified by a 90‑day accelerated dust‑storm test (V‑006c). With M_{\text{dust,jamming}} = 0, the torque margin is 1.67.

Lemma B.1.1a — CFRP Creep‑Rupture Life

The Monkman‑Grant relation t_f \cdot \dot{\epsilon}_{\text{min}}^m = C with C \approx 0.03 and m \approx 1.0 gives t_f > 10^9\ \text{s} (≈ 30 years) at the hinge stress of 50 MPa and Mars UV dose. Stiffness degradation is limited to < 10\% over 3000 sols (RQ‑STR‑003, V‑006).

---

B.2 Micrometeoroid Protection and Film Thickness

Theorem B.2.2b — Hypervelocity Penetration Limit (Theorem 2.2b)

The ballistic limit equation for an FEP film is

t_{\text{req}} \ge k \cdot d_p \left(\frac{\rho_p}{\rho_{\text{FEP}}}\right)^{1/2} \left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2/3}. \tag{B.2}

For a 0.1 mm particle at 20 km/s, t_{\text{req}} \approx 120\ \mu\text{m}. The radiator film is therefore specified at 125 µm (RQ‑MMOD‑001). No single perforation may exceed 0.1% of the total radiating area over the 10‑year mission.

---

B.3 Electrical Crosstalk and Optical Isolation

Lemma B.3.1 — SMA‑VEM High‑Frequency Crosstalk (Lemma B.3.1)

The SMA PWM switching at 1 kHz can capacitively couple to the adjacent VEM ITO electrodes. Instead of heavy low‑pass filters, the design adopts galvanic isolation via optocouplers between the SMA and VEM power buses. Mass penalty: 0.2 kg per panel zone; total 12 kg. RQ‑ELEC‑001 requires isolation resistance > 1 MΩ and VEM voltage ripple < 50 mV during SMA bursts (V‑007).

---

B.4 Nonlinear Smith Predictor for Thermal Control

Theorem B.4.1 — Nonlinear Smith Predictor Stability (Theorem 2.4a)

The radiator thermal dynamics are

\tau_{\text{th}} \frac{dT_s}{dt} = -T_s + T_{\text{eq}}(\Lambda, \dot{m}, V_{\text{VEM}}), \quad \tau_{\text{th}} \approx 300\ \text{s}. \tag{B.3}

The embedded thermocouples have a 10 s lag (T_d = 10\ \text{s}). The Smith predictor uses the full nonlinear function T_{\text{eq}} from Theorem 1.1 as its internal model. Because \partial Q/\partial T_s > 0 everywhere, the predictor is globally monotonically stable. Gain scheduling around the 500 K operating point ensures a phase margin > 60^\circ. The MPC horizon is 600 s with 10 s steps; the robust tube radius is 150 K.

